From 7181532bae791e4fa5d0716228a13aa9537614f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 15:45:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [SDL3-DOS] dos: patch 0146 -- the silence throttle never
 sleeps inside the application's pump

WHY DOS NEEDS THIS: PlayDevice's silence-skip path sleeps 10 ms
(SDL_Delay(10)). That is SDL/0050's quiescence throttle, moved from
GetDeviceBuf into PlayDevice by 0054. It exists to stop the background
playback thread spinning through Iterate while nothing plays. But
PlayDevice is also reached from the APPLICATION's own loop, through
SDL_DOSAudioPump() (SDL/0067). A port that pumps from its game loop
therefore sleeps its game loop 10 ms on every silent buffer. dosags,
which polls once per tick and about once per ms inside its frame wait,
measured it on a 486 (its SDL 0213 message): pump mean 11.0 ms per poll,
and 27.3 stable ticks/s with sound on against 37.5 with sound off.

WHAT: SDL_DOSAudioPump() brackets its Iterate call with a counter,
g_dos_in_app_pump, incremented and decremented under device->lock.
PlayDevice's SDL_Delay(10) runs only when that counter is zero.
  - Every other path, the device thread included, keeps the throttle
    exactly as before, so a port with no application pump sees no
    change.
  - The skip itself is unchanged on every path: the silent buffer is
    still not written, so the Sound Blaster still drains to its idle
    pause.
  - The pump caller paces its own calls, so it needs no throttle from
    the driver.
  - An OpenDevice banner states the rule.
  - A counter rather than a bool, so a nested pump cannot clear it
    early.

Why not thread identity (dosags' first version): device->thread is set
only after SDL_CreateThread returns, and on the cooperative scheduler the
new audio thread runs first. It would see no device thread, skip the
sleep, and -- with SDL/0086's yield killswitched and silence at open --
loop WaitDevice/PlayDevice without sleeping or yielding, so main would
never run again. With the counter, that startup path still sleeps.

What it buys, measured by dosags ("D2", its lab/metric ace672be,
LAB-S32 19.12), on a 486 at 16 bpp:
  - the intermediate metric moved a lot: the pump fell from 8.1 ms to
    0.4 ms;
  - the KPI barely moved: the tick rate went 39.000 -> 39.135 (+0.135,
    against 0.02 N-N noise), and its pre-registered target was
    falsified. The sleep had mostly fallen in idle time; load stalls are
    the remaining limiter.
So land it for correctness of the pump path, not as a frame-rate win.
See docs/optimization.md's worked example.

Validation in the hub (DOSBox-X --sb-live under the host lock, a scratch
probe; nothing queued, so every mixed buffer is silent):
  - Outside the app pump the throttle holds. Device thread alone, 2 s
    idle, before and after the pump calls: 127 silent skips per 2 s
    (63/s) on 0145 and on 0146, identical.
  - Inside the app pump it no longer sleeps. 20 SDL_DOSAudioPump() calls
    on silence: mean 10.73 ms (max 23.81) on 0145, mean 0.64 ms (max
    11.97) on 0146. The one ~12 ms call on 0146 waited for device->lock
    while the device thread was in ITS throttle sleep; the pump call
    itself did not sleep.
  - Audio still plays: SBRING PCM chunk accounting exact (43 and 32), 0
    overwrites across 281200 pump calls, as on 0145.
  - The banner is in the 0146 run's SDL log only.
NOT validated: anything on real hardware, and so the 486 timing -- both
the cost this removes and what it buys come from dosags' own
measurements. The emulator runs show only the mechanism.

Source: dosags patches/SDL/0213 (v2, main e29ef5a6), re-derived against
the hub series. The pump counter and PlayDevice hunks applied cleanly. The
declaration and banner hunks were anchored on dosags-only code (its
NO_BG_THREAD hint and 0144 diagnostic) and were rewritten. The hub banner
states the rule without the dosags-only thread clause. Tags renumbered
0213 -> 0146.

